[](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/8050047cf4381ef77d5569ac23b2adaa6b8484f5be672fd9a52bf011beeb39e8.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104415/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "000408ba-6fbd-51da-9002-82fdde167a1f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104415"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48125/selection/fb96f7aa2c5706bf6efa515ca1ebcb89b9f8fe6d2fe376b7895ba0a9ceeb5e7c" :answer/id #uuid "da74d5a3-27c1-5574-aea9-a73dce167a2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48125/selection/25bc310d867c74fe872781e6992fe6f38d7a1a22fe63f777a66c999ce62ba7b9" :answer/id #uuid "984d002d-b3d9-5fff-85b5-920571715f59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48125/selection/8a66e3a0af3741d9ca55ce958195249f6cfac87457f53f6ba5a52ad61917c3ba" :answer/id #uuid "e8c0e10c-0f05-5103-b9fc-adcc899df027" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48125/selection/e4438c09943b3ab4ee7504916482f54803f71a765439fb27c359efa0cc2a3d00" :answer/id #uuid "a031c657-25d5-51ae-a85c-2e621b1d29e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48125/selection/e47312a2a02ae47543d2a3a6f221adc948e01962c32a23667f2f5e62970b7e14" :answer/id #uuid "30cfde9a-83c4-5a38-bea3-ac4bf8d7ca24" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48125/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e49579b5-6c9a-537b-afe7-9a57de71cbf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48125/selection/e4438c09943b3ab4ee7504916482f54803f71a765439fb27c359efa0cc2a3d00" :answer-field/choices ["q-48125/selection/fb96f7aa2c5706bf6efa515ca1ebcb89b9f8fe6d2fe376b7895ba0a9ceeb5e7c" "q-48125/selection/25bc310d867c74fe872781e6992fe6f38d7a1a22fe63f777a66c999ce62ba7b9" "q-48125/selection/8a66e3a0af3741d9ca55ce958195249f6cfac87457f53f6ba5a52ad61917c3ba" "q-48125/selection/e4438c09943b3ab4ee7504916482f54803f71a765439fb27c359efa0cc2a3d00" "q-48125/selection/e47312a2a02ae47543d2a3a6f221adc948e01962c32a23667f2f5e62970b7e14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48125" :question/id #uuid "a51cbbd3-b785-5c40-be75-e613ee48e2aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-48125" :question/problem "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}+3 & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x=0 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>0\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2(x+1), & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x>0\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+12x+1, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $f(0)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{0}+3=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3(0)+4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=4.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $g(0)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & =2(0+1)=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={0}^{2}+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}g(x)\\ne g(0)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=0$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=0:$\n\n- $h(0)=2(0)^{2}+1=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x) & =2(0)^{2}+1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x) & =(0)^{5}+12(0)+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}h(x)=h(0)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-48125/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48125"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71179/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "ffbf2054-544b-55d2-825d-51d4b8e04592" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71179/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "0b99ee23-faf9-553a-8c85-62f5df9316bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71179/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "13eb3e3b-398e-5705-8b37-faf0d0221d07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71179/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "14c35b4e-8ac4-5203-aa7e-37b1b63cc176" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71179/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "90eb723c-4b84-571c-bd2e-6c5145c1cd52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71179/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "528c1cd4-5920-56e4-a2e9-82178e232930" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71179/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-71179/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-71179/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-71179/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-71179/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-71179/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71179" :question/id #uuid "cc1ce46c-5f51-5585-8b29-6e546ebe7e4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71179" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 13, & x=4,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=g(4)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(4)=13$ exists$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}3x=12, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}3x=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12,$ while the value of the function is $g(4)=13.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)\\ne g(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=4,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-71179/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71179"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104575/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "de29c919-b9b2-5bfd-8e28-8866520f43e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104575/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "6565f2d4-7491-56db-b42e-234169d48802" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104575/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "2597d338-1935-51bc-84a3-21998c272e14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104575/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "82b4ff1d-0067-5205-b166-d2e7e17d665e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104575/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "3251f918-66c1-5662-a1b6-f41a93bab800" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104575/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f518b1-8550-51a9-b688-877ceaaec56d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104575/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-104575/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-104575/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-104575/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-104575/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-104575/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104575" :question/id #uuid "f9a3f2db-4157-50dc-996e-676dd4243a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104575" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-34, & x<-3 \\\\ -25, & x=-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2, & x>-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(-3)=-25$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}({x}^{2}-34)=(-3)^{2}-34=-25, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{+}}({x}^{3}+2)=(-3)^{3}+2=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=-25,$ and the value of the function is $g(-3)=-25.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)=g(-3)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=-3,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104575/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104575"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-71181"] :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x-5, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(1)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(5x-5)=5(1)-5=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={1}^{3}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=0,$ and the value of the function is $g(1)=0.$ So, statement II is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=g(1)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, the function is continuous at $x=0,$ and statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-104572/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "bc0f1e3f-fcd5-5ff6-b081-5cccc4a0ecb2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104572/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "846e0a59-e5c5-5c8a-be6b-2edeaeeb50c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104572/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "95688d2d-06a5-5284-ab99-5c0346344b8f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104572/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "0468e042-4d2f-5f50-88f3-daa1e2261fb8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104572/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e6c7c874-af2c-5ef6-be91-51c99748dd80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104572/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd41f9d3-e177-5f93-88a2-3ef6be301080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104572/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-104572/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-104572/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-104572/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-104572/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-104572/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104572" :question/id #uuid "6d16c9ba-34ea-5f9d-9f33-6f6c8299fea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104572" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+11}, & x<5 \\\\ 12, & x=5 \\\\ {e}^{5/x}, & x>5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(5)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(5)=12$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}\\sqrt{x+11}=4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}{e}^{5/x}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104572/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104572"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3580"] :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a point discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/10ba0496a4dc42579bb95d371a65d808edc0cc5a5653ab3e23d4fe85adf90518.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3579"] :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a removable discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040337/assets/159566da49ef22ea1aae7800355949ae3cf02dac6a6a02e0a8bc344db176a75d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3.$ \n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3581"] :question/problem "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
